---
title: Great horned owl
---
# Great horned owl

The great horned owl is a heavily built owl of the Americas, named for the two
feather tufts that stand above a broad, rufous facial disc. Its diet is the
widest of any American raptor: it is abroad from dusk onward and will take
anything from a beetle to a skunk or a young raccoon. Territories are announced
through the cold months before laying with a deep, resonant series of hoots that
carries a kilometre through still air. It constructs almost nothing of its own,
taking over the old stick platform of a hawk, a squirrel or a heron. See
[[strigidae]].
